1
00:00:07,799 --> 00:00:14,056
The subpoena was issued in February and it was, by
the standards of the Senate, an act

2
00:00:14,076 --> 00:00:16,464
of considerable personal courage.

3
00:00:16,864 --> 00:00:22,609
Senator Grace Kowal of Michigan had spent nineteen
months building a case that a single

4
00:00:22,629 --> 00:00:28,440
private company now held the medical reserves of nine
countries, the diagnostic capacity

5
00:00:28,460 --> 00:00:34,072
of forty-one per cent of American clinical
laboratories, and a longevity therapy that

6
00:00:34,092 --> 00:00:40,102
eleven million Americans were on a waiting list for.
Her staff's report ran to four hundred

7
00:00:40,122 --> 00:00:45,734
pages. It was careful, it was correct in every
particular, and it asked one question:

8
00:00:46,134 --> 00:00:49,404
*What happens to this country if
Umbrella says no?*

9
00:00:49,804 --> 00:00:54,494
She wanted him under oath, on camera, for
four hours, and she got him.

10
00:00:54,895 --> 00:01:00,763
The preparation team was twenty-four strong and had
war-gamed sixty-one questions. On

11
00:01:00,783 --> 00:01:06,305
the last night, Wesker read their briefing book
in nine minutes and put it down.

12
00:01:06,825 --> 00:01:08,915
We will not be using this.

13
00:01:09,314 --> 00:01:12,405
Director, the committee's staff report is
four hundred pages of —

14
00:01:12,805 --> 00:01:17,437
The report is correct. Every number in it is
correct. They have costed the dependency

15
00:01:17,457 --> 00:01:22,473
precisely, they have identified the four failure modes,
and the senator is the first elected

16
00:01:22,493 --> 00:01:26,195
official in eight years to understand what
this company actually is.

17
00:01:26,594 --> 00:01:28,218
Then what's the strategy?

18
00:01:28,617 --> 00:01:29,748
Agree with her.

19
00:01:30,423 --> 00:01:32,233
In front of nine million people?

20
00:01:32,632 --> 00:01:37,888
Nine million people are not going to watch a Senate
hearing, Ms. Adeyemi. Eleven million

21
00:01:37,908 --> 00:01:43,223
are going to watch ninety seconds of it on their
telephones, and I intend to choose which

22
00:01:43,243 --> 00:01:44,123
ninety seconds.

23
00:01:44,522 --> 00:01:47,492
Something behind his eyes turned
over, and spoke.

24
00:01:48,013 --> 00:01:50,011
You have not asked me for anything.

25
00:01:50,409 --> 00:01:51,040
No.

26
00:01:51,441 --> 00:01:57,392
Four hours under oath, hostile chair, nine cameras,
and you have not asked me for one

27
00:01:57,412 --> 00:01:57,814
thing.

28
00:01:58,213 --> 00:02:02,897
Because this is not a crowd. It is eleven people on
a raised bench who have to be seen

29
00:02:02,917 --> 00:02:07,545
to be doing something, a room of a hundred and
forty staffers, and an audience of one

30
00:02:07,565 --> 00:02:12,193
senator. That is not oratory. That is a boardroom
with a flag in it, and I have never

31
00:02:12,213 --> 00:02:13,943
once needed help in a boardroom.

32
00:02:14,674 --> 00:02:16,253
You will need one thing.

33
00:02:16,653 --> 00:02:17,356
Tell me.

34
00:02:17,757 --> 00:02:23,219
When she asks you the question she has spent nineteen
months building — and she will ask

35
00:02:23,239 --> 00:02:28,577
it in the fourth hour, when she believes you are
tired — do not answer it immediately.

36
00:02:28,597 --> 00:02:34,184
Let the room hear the question. Let them realise
nobody has ever asked it out loud before.

37
00:02:34,465 --> 00:02:41,146
The pause is not for you. It is so that eleven million
people understand, before you speak,

38
00:02:41,166 --> 00:02:42,693
that she was *right*.

39
00:02:43,093 --> 00:02:43,943
And then?

40
00:02:44,343 --> 00:02:49,432
And then agree with her, completely, and give her
the thing she is frightened of. You

41
00:02:49,452 --> 00:02:54,540
cannot win that question. You can only refuse to
fight it, in public, with the camera

42
00:02:54,560 --> 00:02:55,020
running.

43
00:02:55,420 --> 00:03:01,690
He came alone. No lawyer at his elbow, no aide
behind him, no folder — a single legal

44
00:03:01,710 --> 00:03:05,093
pad, which he did not write on
for four hours.

45
00:03:05,492 --> 00:03:11,298
The first three hours were exactly what everybody
expected: transfer pricing, acquisition

46
00:03:11,318 --> 00:03:16,862
thresholds, the Delaware contract, the foundation
donations. He answered all of it in

47
00:03:16,882 --> 00:03:22,491
flat, complete sentences without a single instance
of the phrase *I'd have to get back

48
00:03:22,511 --> 00:03:28,317
to you*, and by the second hour two members of the
committee had stopped asking questions

49
00:03:28,337 --> 00:03:33,423
and started asking his opinion, which is the
precise moment a hearing is lost.

50
00:03:33,823 --> 00:03:38,383
At ten past one, Senator Kowal took
the microphone back.

51
00:03:38,902 --> 00:03:44,995
Dr. Wesker, I want to set aside the tax structures,
because frankly they bore me and they

52
00:03:45,015 --> 00:03:46,712
bore the people watching.

53
00:03:46,992 --> 00:03:51,878
In March of last year your company contained an
outbreak in sixty-one days that every

54
00:03:51,898 --> 00:03:56,900
model said would kill between two and eleven million
people. You did it free of charge.

55
00:03:56,920 --> 00:04:02,152
You did it faster than the World Health Organization,
faster than my own government, faster

56
00:04:02,172 --> 00:04:04,922
than anyone has ever done
anything of that kind.

57
00:04:05,203 --> 00:04:10,660
And I have spent nineteen months trying to find a way
to say thank you for that and still

58
00:04:10,680 --> 00:04:13,552
do my job. So let me just ask
you the question.

59
00:04:14,108 --> 00:04:18,177
What happens to the United States of
America if you decide not to?

60
00:04:19,348 --> 00:04:24,686
The narrator would like you to hear the length of
that silence, because the transcript

61
00:04:24,706 --> 00:04:30,044
records it as twenty-three seconds and the video is
the most-watched ninety seconds of

62
00:04:30,064 --> 00:04:32,038
political footage of the decade.

63
00:04:32,437 --> 00:04:38,602
Albert Wesker sat at a plain table in a room with nine
cameras in it, and he did not reach

64
00:04:38,622 --> 00:04:44,512
for the microphone, and he did not look at his legal
pad, and the room got quieter and

65
00:04:44,532 --> 00:04:50,352
quieter as a hundred and forty staffers realised
that nobody in the building had ever

66
00:04:50,372 --> 00:04:52,757
said that sentence out loud before.

67
00:04:53,278 --> 00:04:57,508
You are right to ask, and the answer is that
it would be a catastrophe.

68
00:04:58,293 --> 00:05:01,963
Somewhere at the back of the room, a
photographer stopped shooting.

69
00:05:02,483 --> 00:05:07,293
If Umbrella withdrew from the stockpile contracts
tomorrow, this country would have no

70
00:05:07,313 --> 00:05:12,179
strategic medical reserve for nineteen months. If we
ceased assay production, forty-one

71
00:05:12,199 --> 00:05:17,009
per cent of your clinical laboratory capacity would
go dark in six weeks and could not

72
00:05:17,029 --> 00:05:21,839
be rebuilt in under three years. If we stopped
Aeterna, eleven million Americans would

73
00:05:21,859 --> 00:05:26,725
come off a therapy that their bodies have adapted
to, and the mortality consequences of

74
00:05:26,745 --> 00:05:31,556
that are in your own staff's report on page three
hundred and eleven, and they are not

75
00:05:31,576 --> 00:05:31,893
small.

76
00:05:32,173 --> 00:05:37,410
Senator, you have described the dependency accurately,
and I will not insult this committee

77
00:05:37,430 --> 00:05:43,014
by pretending it is not real, or that it was accidental,
or that I did not build it deliberately,

78
00:05:43,034 --> 00:05:43,823
because I did.

79
00:05:44,223 --> 00:05:50,037
A member of the committee began to speak. Kowal
held up one hand without looking away

80
00:05:50,057 --> 00:05:51,204
from the witness.

81
00:05:51,723 --> 00:05:54,398
So let us discuss what to do about it.

82
00:05:54,797 --> 00:05:56,897
And then he gave away the second thing.

83
00:05:57,416 --> 00:06:02,700
First: the stockpile contracts. Umbrella will accept
a statutory provision, drafted by

84
00:06:02,720 --> 00:06:08,559
this committee, placing our domestic reserve under
federal requisition in a declared emergency.

85
00:06:08,579 --> 00:06:13,801
Not by negotiation. By *seizure*, at a price you
set in advance. We will not litigate

86
00:06:13,821 --> 00:06:16,947
it and we will not require
compensation above cost.

87
00:06:17,226 --> 00:06:21,875
Second: the assay platform has been in an open
licence since 2029. It is free. What is

88
00:06:21,895 --> 00:06:26,489
not free is the manufacturing, and that is the real
dependency, so Umbrella will fund

89
00:06:26,509 --> 00:06:31,103
— at our expense, on land we do not own, operated
by companies we have no interest in

90
00:06:31,123 --> 00:06:35,771
— three cartridge plants in this country, whose
output we will never take a cent from,

91
00:06:35,791 --> 00:06:37,617
and whose designs we will publish.

92
00:06:37,896 --> 00:06:42,586
Third, and I will be very plain about this one,
because it is the one you actually want.

93
00:06:43,197 --> 00:06:48,061
Aeterna. The compound is under patent until 2046. I
will place the manufacturing process

94
00:06:48,081 --> 00:06:52,780
in escrow with the Department of Health and Human
Services, under a trigger that this

95
00:06:52,800 --> 00:06:57,554
committee writes, and that trigger can include a
determination by a federal court that

96
00:06:57,574 --> 00:07:02,439
Umbrella has withheld supply. If I ever say no, you
take it. All of it. Permanently, and

97
00:07:02,459 --> 00:07:03,826
without paying me for it.

98
00:07:04,226 --> 00:07:09,983
The narrator will tell you the number, because
Wesker had it in front of him and nobody

99
00:07:10,003 --> 00:07:15,958
on the committee did: the escrow provision put
approximately two hundred and forty billion

100
00:07:15,978 --> 00:07:21,867
dollars of future revenue inside a box with the key
held by his opponents, and he offered

101
00:07:21,887 --> 00:07:26,117
it in a flat voice as though he were
conceding a point of order.

102
00:07:26,637 --> 00:07:28,087
Why would you do that?

103
00:07:28,487 --> 00:07:32,969
Because you were right, Senator, and the
alternative is that this arrangement depends

104
00:07:32,989 --> 00:07:37,525
on my goodwill, and no country should ever have to
depend on the goodwill of a private

105
00:07:37,545 --> 00:07:37,737
man.

106
00:07:38,456 --> 00:07:40,627
Including me. *Especially* me.

107
00:07:41,026 --> 00:07:43,437
The clip was up before he left
the building.

108
00:07:43,956 --> 00:07:48,369
(broadcast) — the chief executive of the largest
company in human history, under oath,

109
00:07:48,389 --> 00:07:52,801
agreeing with a hostile senator that his own company
is too powerful, and then handing

110
00:07:52,821 --> 00:07:57,490
over the keys. I have covered this town for twenty-two
years and I have never seen anything

111
00:07:57,510 --> 00:07:58,367
remotely like it.

112
00:07:58,766 --> 00:08:03,286
(broadcast) The cynical read is that he gave away
things he never expected to lose. The

113
00:08:03,306 --> 00:08:07,616
escrow only triggers if he withholds supply, and
why would he ever withhold supply?

114
00:08:08,016 --> 00:08:12,910
(broadcast) That's the cynical read. The other read is
that he's the only person in Washington

115
00:08:12,930 --> 00:08:15,367
today who answered the question
that was asked.

116
00:08:15,766 --> 00:08:22,199
Senator Grace Kowal's bill passed in June with
eighty-one votes. It is still on the statute

117
00:08:22,219 --> 00:08:25,177
book. The escrow has never been triggered.

118
00:08:25,576 --> 00:08:31,768
Her committee's staff report — four hundred pages,
nineteen months, entirely correct —

119
00:08:31,788 --> 00:08:38,414
was cited in fourteen editorials that week as the
document that had forced the most powerful

120
00:08:38,434 --> 00:08:43,037
man alive to disarm himself, and she was
re-elected twice on it.

121
00:08:43,437 --> 00:08:51,499
She retired in 2041, and in 2044 she took Aeterna, and
she wrote about the decision honestly

122
00:08:51,519 --> 00:08:54,047
in a memoir that nobody read.

123
00:08:55,197 --> 00:09:03,381
Devotion, institutional. Audience: one hundred and
forty-one million. Approval among United

124
00:09:03,401 --> 00:09:11,495
States adults: seventy-nine per cent. Legislative risk
reduced for approximately seventeen

125
00:09:11,515 --> 00:09:19,338
years. One million nine hundred thousand Points.
Level sixty-six. Presence: thirty-four

126
00:09:19,358 --> 00:09:20,510
to forty-six.

127
00:09:21,100 --> 00:09:26,731
Senator Grace Kowal watched her own hearing that
night, alone, with the sound off for

128
00:09:26,751 --> 00:09:32,581
the last twenty minutes, and then telephoned the one
person she had promised herself she

129
00:09:32,601 --> 00:09:33,911
would not telephone.

130
00:09:34,311 --> 00:09:40,594
Her chief of staff's note of the call is four lines
and was published in her papers in

131
00:09:40,614 --> 00:09:40,961
2061.

132
00:09:41,480 --> 00:09:46,695
He gave me everything I asked for. Everything. The
requisition clause, the plants, the

133
00:09:46,715 --> 00:09:51,991
escrow. My bill will pass with eighty votes and it
will be the biggest thing I ever do.

134
00:09:52,270 --> 00:09:57,887
And I have been sitting here for two hours trying to
work out why I feel as though I have

135
00:09:57,907 --> 00:10:03,270
just been *handled*, and the answer is that every
single one of those concessions was

136
00:10:03,290 --> 00:10:08,844
drafted before I subpoenaed him. The escrow
instrument is forty-one pages. Nobody writes

137
00:10:08,864 --> 00:10:10,681
forty-one pages in a hearing.

138
00:10:11,400 --> 00:10:16,210
(very slow) He came to Washington with the
concessions in his pocket and waited for me

139
00:10:16,230 --> 00:10:20,870
to ask the right question so that he could hand
them over as though I had won them.

140
00:10:21,150 --> 00:10:26,169
And here is the part I cannot get past. If I had
asked a worse question, he would have

141
00:10:26,189 --> 00:10:31,208
kept them in his pocket, and my bill would have been
nothing, and he would have let me

142
00:10:31,228 --> 00:10:31,500
fail.

143
00:10:31,780 --> 00:10:36,670
(very slow) He made me earn it. Do you understand
what I am saying? The most powerful

144
00:10:36,690 --> 00:10:41,579
man alive made the United States Senate *earn* a
concession he had already decided to

145
00:10:41,599 --> 00:10:45,911
give, because he thinks we are better at
holding things we had to work for.

146
00:10:46,191 --> 00:10:51,600
He is right about that. That is what I cannot get
past. He is right, and it is the most

147
00:10:51,620 --> 00:10:57,093
patronising thing that has ever been done to me, and
I am going to spend the next twenty

148
00:10:57,113 --> 00:10:58,841
years being grateful for it.

149
00:10:59,240 --> 00:11:02,370
The voice that was not his came up
out of the quiet.

150
00:11:02,890 --> 00:11:05,963
Two hundred and forty billion dollars.

151
00:11:06,365 --> 00:11:06,987
Yes.

152
00:11:07,386 --> 00:11:13,058
You gave away the mechanism of your own supremacy in
this country to a woman who intended

153
00:11:13,078 --> 00:11:15,807
to destroy you, and she
thanked you for it.

154
00:11:16,207 --> 00:11:21,023
I gave away a *lock*, Adolf. Not a door. The escrow
triggers if I withhold supply. I have

155
00:11:21,043 --> 00:11:25,805
no intention of withholding supply, ever, from
anyone, because withholding supply is the

156
00:11:25,825 --> 00:11:30,532
crude instrument of a man who has nothing else — and
now every legislature on earth has

157
00:11:30,552 --> 00:11:35,259
watched the United States Senate extract the one
concession that they will all ask for,

158
00:11:35,279 --> 00:11:36,997
and get, and consider a victory.

159
00:11:37,398 --> 00:11:38,863
You have made a template.

160
00:11:39,263 --> 00:11:44,139
I have made a template, and I wrote it myself, and it
will be copied in eleven parliaments

161
00:11:44,159 --> 00:11:48,873
